>> title: Sumac Onions
>> category: Toppings & Pickles
>> tags: onion, sumac, parsley, garnish, no-cook
>> counters: Shawarma Counter, The Bowl Shop
>> aka: sumac onions, sumak sogan, soganli salata, piyaz, onion sumac salad, sumac onion garnish, red onion with sumac
>> pairs-with: pita-bread, tabbouleh, mujaddara
>> servings: 6
>> time: 35 min
>> step.1: slice paper-thin, rinse cold
>> step.2: salt and drain 15 min
>> step.3: toss with sumac, parsley and lemon
>> step.4: stand 10 min

Slice @red onion{1%large}(200 g) pole to pole as thin as you can make it and rinse the slices under cold running water for a full minute. Pole to pole rather than across: the slices hold their shape instead of collapsing, and the rinse takes off the sulphurous edge that makes raw onion hang around all afternoon.

Toss @&(~1)sliced onion{} with @kosher salt{1/2%tsp} and ~drain{15%min} in a sieve, then press the water out with the flat of your hand.

Toss @ground sumac{1%Tbs}(7 g), @flat-leaf parsley{1/4%cup}(chopped, stems and all), @lemon juice{1%Tbs}(15 mL) and @extra-virgin olive oil{1%Tbs}(15 mL) through @&(~1)drained onion{} until every slice is stained purple-red.

Let @&(~1)sumac onions{} ~stand{10%min} before serving, so the sumac goes from powdery to wet. This lands on every plate that comes off the spit, and it is doing a job — it is the sharp thing that cuts the fat, not decoration.
